// Pimble Cloud accounts service schema (docs/CLOUD_CONTRACT.md, section C:
// "Identity and grants").
//
// Deviations from the contract's literal field list, both deliberate:
//
// 1. `User.user_uuid` is the JWT `sub` — a UUID minted by this service at
//    signup (never RhypeDB's own internal `id`, which is an implementation
//    detail of the database and not something we hand out or accept back
//    from a client). Likewise `HostedStore.store_id` is the Pimble server's
//    own `StoreId` (also a UUID), not RhypeDB's internal id.
//
// 2. `Session.user_rid`, `Grant.user_rid`/`Grant.store_rid`/`Grant.store_uuid`
//    are redundant scalar copies of what the `user`/`store` relations already
//    say. RhypeDB's query language has no relation-equality filter — reading
//    rhypedb-query's parser and executor (`Predicate::Compare`'s right-hand
//    side is always a scalar `Literal`; `evaluate_predicate` reads the
//    comparison field straight out of the object's own FieldMap, which never
//    contains a relation) confirms `.filter(.store == HostedStore.get(1))` is
//    not a thing today, and neither is `@unique` across a pair of relations.
//    Without these scalar copies, "does a grant already exist for (user,
//    store)", "list a store's members", and "build this user's JWT `stores`
//    claim" would each need a relation traversal per candidate row instead of
//    one scalar-filtered query. The `user`/`store` relations stay for
//    referential correctness (and are what a future phase's `@on_delete`
//    policies would hang off); the scalars are a query-performance shortcut,
//    not the source of truth.

type User {
    user_uuid: String @unique
    email: String
    email_lower: String @unique
    // Argon2id(auth_key) since phase 2a (docs/CRYPTO_CONTRACT.md): the field
    // and the hashing are unchanged from phase 1b's password_hash, only what
    // gets hashed changed (the server never sees a password any more).
    password_hash: String
    created_at: DateTime
    // Phase 1b (email verification): unverified at signup. `verify_token_hash`
    // is sha256(opaque verify token), empty string once verified or replaced
    // by a newer token (an empty query token is rejected before it would ever
    // match this sentinel — see routes/accounts.rs::verify). `verify_expires_at`
    // is 24h from when the token was (re)issued.
    verified: Bool
    verify_token_hash: String
    verify_expires_at: DateTime
    // Phase 2a (docs/CRYPTO_CONTRACT.md "Data model additions"): every field
    // below is required from signup on — there is no keyless `User` row any
    // more (the two pre-phase-2a smoke accounts are deleted on migration; see
    // this crate's README). `kdf_*` and `public_*` are plain scalars;
    // `account_key_blob` and `recovery_key_blob` are `pimble_crypto::
    // AccountKeyBlob` serialized to a JSON string (opaque to this schema,
    // exactly like `KeyGrant.envelope` below).
    kdf_salt: String
    kdf_m_cost: u32
    kdf_t_cost: u32
    kdf_p_cost: u32
    public_encryption_key: String
    public_signing_key: String
    account_key_blob: String
    recovery_salt: String
    recovery_key_blob: String
    // Phase 2a-2 (docs/CRYPTO_CONTRACT.md "account recovery, password
    // change, new recovery code"): `POST /recover/start`'s emailed token,
    // sha256'd, one hour, one use — same sentinel-on-absent-or-consumed
    // pattern as `verify_token_hash` above. Absent on every row from before
    // this addition (read as optional; see db.rs's `get_string_opt`/
    // `get_datetime_ms_opt` callers in `user_from_object`), which is
    // correct: no in-flight recovery to speak of for a row that predates
    // the field.
    recovery_token_hash: String
    recovery_token_expires_at: DateTime
}

type Session {
    token_hash: String @unique
    user: User
    user_rid: u64 @indexed
    expires_at: DateTime
    created_at: DateTime
}

type HostedStore {
    store_id: String @unique
    name: String
    dir_name: String
    created_at: DateTime
    deleted: Bool
    // Phase 2a (docs/CRYPTO_CONTRACT.md): `"plain"` or `"vault"`, matching
    // `pimble_core::StoreKind`'s serde spelling. Kept as a plain String like
    // every other field here rather than an enum, since this schema has no
    // enum type and the Pimble server is the source of truth for what the
    // string means. Absent on every row created before Phase 2a — read
    // optional (`hosted_store_from_object`, defaulting to `"plain"`), like
    // every field added after a generation shipped.
    kind: String
    // Dead since docs/NODE_DOCUMENT_CONTRACT.md section 5: a share was
    // briefly a vault store of its own (`share: true`), and is now a scoped
    // grant on the owner's own store — `Grant.root` below. Nothing writes
    // this field any more and nothing reads it (`hosted_store_from_object`
    // ignores it), but production rows from that cut still carry it, so the
    // declaration stays: a field a stored row has and the schema does not is
    // not a shape this database has ever been asked to load.
    share: Bool
}

// An address invited to a store that has no verified account yet. One row per
// (store, email_lower, root), enforced in the service (find-then-create/update)
// like `Grant`'s (user, store, root) — see this file's header comment on why
// relation-pair uniqueness isn't a schema-level thing here. An invitation
// becomes a `Grant` and is deleted the moment the address is claimed (at
// verification and at every login), so nothing here is ever read for an
// account that exists.
//
// `store_rid`/`store_uuid` are the same denormalized scalar copies `Grant`
// carries, for the same reason. `email_lower` is what the claim path queries.
// `invited_by_rid` is a scalar with no matching relation on purpose: the
// inviter is only ever read back to name them in the invitation mail, and a
// row whose inviter has since deleted their account is still a perfectly good
// invitation (the lookup simply finds nothing and the mail says the store's
// name instead).
type Invitation {
    store: HostedStore
    store_rid: u64 @indexed
    store_uuid: String
    email: String
    email_lower: String @indexed
    role: String
    // The scope this invitation is for, exactly like `Grant.root` below: the
    // empty string for the whole store, a node id for a share. Carried into
    // the grant when the address is claimed. Absent on every row written
    // before shares became scopes — read optional, defaulting to the whole
    // store, which is what those rows really are.
    root: String
    // What the owner called this share, exactly like `Grant.share_name`
    // below, and carried into the grant with `root` when the address is
    // claimed. Empty for a whole-store invitation (the store has a name of
    // its own). Read optional, like every field added after rows existed.
    share_name: String
    invited_by_rid: u64
    created_at: DateTime
}

type Grant {
    user: User
    store: HostedStore
    role: String
    // docs/NODE_DOCUMENT_CONTRACT.md section 5: a grant has a scope. The
    // empty string is the whole store (every grant before shares became
    // scopes); a node id is a share — this user holds `role` on that node and
    // the documents under it, and on nothing else in the store. One row per
    // (user, store, root), enforced in the service. Absent on every row
    // written before this field existed, read optional as the whole store.
    root: String
    // What the owner called this share (Joe, 2026-09-21: "a share has a name
    // of its own"): the name a recipient's store list shows, so the owner's
    // store name never reaches them. Empty for a whole-store grant, which is
    // named by its store. Every grant and invitation of one (store, root)
    // carries the same name — `PUT members` rewrites them all — so a share
    // has one name however many people hold it. Absent on every row written
    // before this field existed; read optional, like every later field.
    share_name: String
    user_rid: u64 @indexed
    store_rid: u64 @indexed
    store_uuid: String
}

// Phase 2a (docs/CRYPTO_CONTRACT.md "Data model additions"): one row per
// (user, store, key_id, scope_root) — a store's key rotates by adding a new
// key_id and new envelopes per member, never by mutating an old row (old blobs
// still need their old envelope to decrypt). That uniqueness is enforced in
// the service, like `Grant`'s `(user, store, root)` — see schema.rhype's
// header comment on why relation-pair uniqueness isn't a schema-level thing
// here.
type KeyGrant {
    user: User
    store: HostedStore
    key_id: String
    // Which scope key this envelope carries (docs/NODE_DOCUMENT_CONTRACT.md
    // section 5): the empty string for the store key, a node id for that
    // share's key. Absent on every row from before shares became scopes —
    // read optional as the store key, which every such envelope holds.
    scope_root: String
    // A `pimble_crypto::KeyEnvelope` serialized to a JSON string — opaque to
    // this schema and to RhypeDB, exactly like `User.account_key_blob`.
    envelope: String
    user_rid: u64 @indexed
    store_rid: u64 @indexed
    store_uuid: String
}
